{
  "$comment": "Every commit of this repository that a public record outside it cites by ID, translated across the history rewrite of 2026-09-18. That rewrite ran git filter-repo over every branch and tag and force-pushed the result, so every commit received a new ID and the old IDs stopped resolving in a clone. A record that names an old ID can use this file to find the commit it meant. The file lists only commits cited somewhere public (a run record, a pinned document, an issue or pull request comment), not every commit the rewrite touched. How each row was checked: the old commit was read through the GitHub API, which still served every one of them on 2026-09-24, and the rewritten commit was read in a fresh clone with GIT_NO_LAZY_FETCH=1. For every row the commit message and author date are unchanged, each parent maps through the rewrite to the rewritten commit's parent, and the vendored specification under spec/ is unchanged. vectorsTree compares the corpus directory: identical means the same tree object, byte for byte. Where it is not identical, vectorsDifferences names each file that differs and what changed in it. tag is the annotated tag cited/<first seven characters of the old ID> that keeps the rewritten commit reachable, so a clone always carries it; it is null where no record pins the commit as a suite revision. scripts/suite-commit-gate.py checks that every rewritten ID here resolves in a clone and that every tag named here points at its row.",
  "rewrite": {
    "date": "2026-09-18",
    "tool": "git filter-repo",
    "scope": "every branch and every tag"
  },
